            <ArticleTitle>Plasma beta-endorphin levels in oral surgery patients following diazepam, fentanyl or placebo.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>124-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Plasma beta-endorphin, pain and anxiety were measured in patients before, during, and 1 and 3 hours following oral surgery. Diazepam and fentanyl blocked the stress induced increase in plasma beta-endorphin experienced by patients administered placebo. Moreover, intra-operative anxiety and post-operative pain appear to constitute independent and possibly equipotent stimuli for release of pituitary beta-endorphin in humans.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
